--TEST--
A `Core\Process\Handle` that a child task returns names no process in the task that receives it, so calling it there stops the program and leaves that task's own process alone — `rule:core-classes/process-spawn`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: Core\Process::spawn
// A task returns a handle, and `kill()` on it outside the task stops the program.
// That call does not stop the program's own process, so it is reported at the end.

string $path = "/bin/cat";
if (Core\Env::OS == Core\Env::Platform::Windows) {
    $path = "C:\\Windows\\System32\\sort.exe";
}
Core\Process\Handle $own = Core\Process::spawn($path, []);
var $result = Core\Task::all({
    handle: fn (): Core\Process\Handle => {
        string $path = "/bin/cat";
        if (Core\Env::OS == Core\Env::Platform::Windows) {
            $path = "C:\\Windows\\System32\\sort.exe";
        }
        Core\Process\Handle $child = Core\Process::spawn($path, []);
        $child->kill();
        return $child;
    },
});
echo "the task returned its handle\n";
$result->handle->kill();
echo "this line does not run\n";
--FILE nvs.toml--
[capabilities.process]
exec = true
--EXPECT--
the task returned its handle
--EXPECTF-ERROR--
FATAL: `Core\Process\Handle::kill` was called on a handle from another task. A handle works only in the task that called `Core\Process::spawn`.
{"level":"error","msg":"A process started with `Core\\Process::spawn` was still running when the program ended, so it was stopped. Call `wait()` or `kill()` on its handle before the program ends.","fields":{"program":"%s"}}
